## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly security thinking around user-generated content in a chat app; it covers validation, malware scanning, storage isolation, and user feedback.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation: MIME vs extension, size caps
- Safe storage: per-user paths, UUIDs, signed URLs
- Malware scanning: pre-publish check and quarantine
- Access control and auditing: per-object ACLs, immutable logs

## Code Example
```javascript
const MAX_SIZE = 5 * 1024 * 1024; // 5 MB
const ALLOWED = new Set(['image/png','image/jpeg','application/pdf']);
app.post('/upload', (req,res)=>{
  const f = req.file;
  if (!f || f.size > MAX_SIZE) return res.status(400).send('Invalid size');
  if (!ALLOWED.has(f.mimetype)) return res.status(400).send('Unsupported type');
  const key = `uploads/${user.id}/${uuid()}${ext(f.originalname)}`;
  // upload to storage, then scan
  storage.put(key, f.buffer).then(()=> scanService.scan(key)).then(r=>{
    if (r.clean) return res.json({url: signer.sign(key)});
    // on fail
    storage.delete(key); res.status(400).send('malware detected');
  }).catch(()=> res.status(500).send('error'));
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle scalable malware scanning for peak traffic?
- What changes would you make for privacy when scanning contents?
- How would you implement a rollback path if a user re-uploads a clean file after a scan failure?

## Why This Is Asked
Webhooks are a common attack surface in multi-tenant systems. This question tests understanding of edge verification, per-tenant key management, and robust replay protection under high throughput.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant HMAC signing with rotated secrets stored in HSM (AWS CloudHSM, Azure Key Vault)
- Replay protection via timestamp + nonce sliding window (5 minutes default)
- Secret rotation (automatic, 90-day policy) and revocation with auditable logs
- Edge validation, TLS 1.3, and tamper-evident delivery
- Incident response procedures for key compromise including tenant notification and emergency revocation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js: verify webhook signature at edge
const crypto = require('crypto');

function verify(payload, timestamp, nonce, signature, secret) {
  const age = Date.now() - timestamp;
  if (age > 5 * 60 * 1000) throw new Error('Replay window exceeded');

  const expected = crypto
    .createHmac('sha256', secret)
    .update(`${timestamp}.${nonce}.${payload}`)
    .digest('hex');

  return crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(signature), Buffer.from(expected));
}
```

## Trade-offs
- Redis vs DynamoDB: Redis offers lower latency but less durability; DynamoDB provides ACID compliance with higher latency
- HSM vs KMS: HSM offers superior security certification (FIPS 140-2 Level 3) but higher cost; KMS is more cost-effective but provides software-based protection
